 wins the rook on c1 next move, because White's rook is undefended.  \n- Even if White blocks with the bishop on d3 (Bd3-e4), then Nxe4 still wins a piece.  \n- This move creates immediate threats, forks the king and rook, and forces White into material loss while keeping initiative.  \n\nOther moves like Bf5-d3 or Nc3-a2 are less decisive and allow White time to consolidate or counterattack.  \n\n**Answer:** N: c3-e2"}
