taneously (fork), likely winning the rook if White doesn’t defend properly.  \n- **B: f5-e4** — Pins the white knight on b3(?) (Actually no, B to e4 attacks d3 and f3 squares; not a pin yet but puts pressure toward king’s position).  \n- **N: h7-f6** — Develops knight toward center, but less forcing.  \n- **P: b7-b5** — Gains space on queenside and prepares b5-b4 if needed.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nThe knight fork **e2-c3** is a tactical shot that immediately wins material (rook on e1) unless White can defend — and in the current position, White can only move the king, but then the rook is lost. The move is safe because the knight is protected by the bishop on f5. This is the most forcing and highest reward move, shifting material advantage to Black.\n\n**Answer:** N: e2-c3'}
