s discoveries and supports future attacks.\n\nReason: N: f5-e3 is very strong because it directly attacks the white king, which is on f3. White must respond to the check, and after the king moves (likely to e3? Wait, e3 is where knight goes, so king must move to e2, g2, or f2), Black can follow up with further attacks. The knight on e3 is also well-placed for coordination with the rook. This move creates immediate pressure and capitalizes on Black's active pieces and the exposed white king.\n\nAnswer: N: f5-e3"}
