.  \n\nOur valid moves are only King moves from f7: K: f7-e6, f7-e7, f7-e8, f7-f8, f7-g6, f7-g7, f7-g8.\n\nEvaluation:  \n- White’s immediate threat: Pawn g6 is one square from promotion (g7 is empty, g8 is empty). If we don’t move to stop it, White could push g6-g7 next turn, and then g7-g8=Queen, which could be dangerous despite our material advantage.  \n- Our king is currently on f7, which is adjacent to g6 (one square diagonally f7-g6 is a possible capture).  \n- We must consider:  \n  1. Capturing the g6 pawn with K: f7-g6. This removes the immediate promotion threat.  \n  2. Moving king to g7 (f7-g7) blocks the pawn’s advance (pawn on g6 cannot move forward because g7 is occupied).  \n  3. Moving king away (e6, e7, e8, f8) leaves g6 pawn free to advance.\n\nClearly, the most urgent is to stop the g-pawn. Let’s evaluate each option:\n\n1. K: f7-g6 – captures the pawn, removes it entirely. But then White king may try to attack our king? White king at f3 is far, not an immediate threat. However, our king on g6 might be a bit exposed, but White has only king and one pawn left (g5). The pawn on g5 can then move g5-g4, but not an immediate promotion threat.  \n   Also, after Kxg6, White’s only move likely is Kg3 or something, then we can bring our rook/knights to help checkmate.  \n\n2. K: f7-g7 – blocks the pawn, but pawn remains. Then White could play g5-g4, still two pawns. Our king blocks pawn advance, but we haven’t captured it. We could later capture with king or with other pieces. However, g7 might be safer than g6? Actually g7 is one rank closer to our back rank, but that doesn’t matter much.  \n\n3. Other moves like Kf7-e6, e7, e8, f8 all allow g6-g7 next, and then g7-g8=Q, giving White a queen, making the game complicated. We have a rook and knights, but a queen can cause checks and possibly draw. We should avoid that.\n\nBetween g6 and g7:  \n- g6 captures pawn, reduces White’s pawns to one.  \n- g7 blocks but pawn remains; White might try Kh4 or something to support pawn push later. But with our rook and knights, we can still coordinate.  \n\nThe safest and most forcing is to capture the pawn (g6). That eliminates the promotion threat entirely and leaves White with only king and one pawn far from promotion. Then we can slowly coordinate our pieces to checkmate.\n\nReason:  \nThe immediate danger is the g6 pawn promoting. Capturing it with K: f7-g6 removes that danger, simplifies the position, and uses our king actively. After capturing, White’s king is far away and we can centralize our rook and knights to push for checkmate. Moving to g7 only blocks, but the pawn remains and might later be pushed with support, causing complications. So capturing is more decisive.\n\nAnswer: K: f7-g6"}
