e or block.  \n3. **R: d7-d4** — Puts rook on same rank as White’s king at h4, could pin something later or create rook lift threats.  \n4. **K: g6-f5** — Moving king toward center/away from potential pawn advance g5-g6. Could be risky with king exposed.  \n5. **P: c4-c3** — Pawn advance, opens lines? Not urgent.\n\nBest immediate chance: **N: e3-g4** is a check, forcing White’s king to move. White’s only legal moves with king: h3, h5, g3. All awkward; could lead to further attacks or fork opportunities.\n\n**Reason:**  \nWe want to seize the initiative. White’s king is exposed on h4; delivering check with knight restricts White’s options and could allow follow-up tactics (rook to h7, knight fork later, etc.). Compared to other moves, this directly pressures the opponent’s king, which aligns with maximizing winning chances in an open position.\n\n**Answer:** N: e3-g4'}
