ing pawns (a6-a5 or c4-c3) are slow and don’t address immediate mating chances.\n\nPromising moves:  \n1. **N: g4-e3+** – Immediate check, forces white king to move (likely to h3 or f3), then Black can follow up with rook or knight moves to continue attack.  \n2. **N: c6-e5** – Attacks pawn on g5 and eyes f3/d3 squares near white king; no immediate check but increases pressure.  \n3. **K: g6-f5** – Centralizes king, threatens to capture g5 pawn next move if not defended, and approaches white king.\n\nReason:  \nN: g4-e3+ is the most forcing move. It puts the white king in check and forces it to a more confined position. Since White has only king and pawn, Black can use the initiative to coordinate rook and knights for a quick mate. The knight check also potentially uncovers an attack from the rook on d7 (though not immediately). After Ke3/h3/f3, Black can consider moves like Rd3+ or Nc6-e5 attacking the pawn and further limiting white king’s squares. It’s direct and aggressive, maximizing immediate pressure.\n\nAnswer: N: g4-e3'}
