cks white king directly (discovered check? No, but fork of king and pawn? Actually N: e3-g4 attacks king on f4 and pawn on g5 simultaneously).  \n3. **N: c6-e5** – Threatens knight fork (Nxe5?? Actually Nc6-e5 attacks king on f4 and pawn on g5 as well, plus rook covers).  \n\nReason:  \nR: d7-d4 is strongest because it’s a direct check from the rook, forcing the white king to move and likely into a more cramped position. After Kf4 moves (e.g., to g4), then N: e3-g2+ could be a follow-up check, or N: c6-e5+ could deliver another check. This forces the king into a mating net quickly. Other knight moves don’t give immediate check, so the rook check is most forcing and accelerates the win.  \n\nAnswer: R: d7-d4"}
