gest forcing move — it puts White in check, forcing a king move and likely gaining time to reposition our other pieces. Since White has only pawn moves (none here) or king moves to respond, we keep the initiative. With rook and knight, we can gradually box the white king toward a corner while advancing pawns.  \nOther moves are less direct. Knight moves allow White’s king more freedom, and king moves are slow. In rook-and-knight vs. king endgames, cutting off the king with the rook is key.\n\nAnswer: R: d2-d3'}
