n 1 left? h6 to g4 is 2 down 1 left, so yes! That’s a knight move: h6→g4 is -2 rank, -1 file. So Nf6? Wait, h6 to g4: yes. So Nh6 attacks g4. That’s not check yet but threatens discovered check by moving king.  \n\nBetter direct: **N: g4-f2** — from f2 attacks h3 and g4, not h4. So not check.  \n\nBut consider: If we move K: f4-g5, not in valid moves. Not allowed.  \n\nFrom valid moves, best might be **K: f4-e5** — centralizes king and prepares Kg5 next move with knight support for mate. After Ke5, White king’s moves: h5 (attacked by our knight from g4), g5 (attacked by our king from e5), h3, g3. h5 is attacked by knight, so White might play Kh3. Then we play Nf2+! forcing Kg2 or Kh2, then mate soon.\n\nBut let’s check immediate tactical blow:  \n**N: g4-f6** — now from f6, knight attacks h5 and g4? h5 yes, g4 no. After Nh5 by White? If White king moves to h5, then our Kf4?? Wait Kf4 to g3? Not possible.  \n\nActually simpler: **K: f4-f5** — now White king h4, if Kh5?? Then our Nf2 is not possible (knight still on g4). We can play Nf6+! Actually from g4 to f6 is check? No, from g4 to f6 attacks h5? Yes, if king on h5, Nf6 is check! So sequence: 1. Kf5 Kh5 2. Nf6# — checkmate! Because from f6 knight attacks h5 (king’s square) and king on f5 covers g5 and g6, h5 has no escape.  \n\nSo Kf5 forces Kh5 (only legal move besides maybe h3 but h3 is also available). If Kh3 instead of Kh5 after Kf5, then we play Nf2+ (discovered check from Kf5) and then mate follows.  \n\nThus Kf5 is winning immediately.\n\n**Reason:**  \nKf5 creates an inescapable mating net in at most two moves. If Kh5, Nf6#. If Kh3, Nf2+ (discovered check) forces Kg2 or Kh2, then our king approaches for mate. The pawns can wait.\n\n**Answer:** K: f4-f5'}
