ally placed to stop it immediately. Moving it to c2 creates a passed pawn that will require White's King to divert attention, allowing Black's King to advance and assist. The move **P: c3-c2** is simple and strong, advancing the pawn one step closer to queening. Black's King at f5 is well-placed to support promotion on the queenside, and the Knight can also help later. Other moves like K: f5-e4 or N: g4-e3 are also good, but advancing the pawn immediately forces White to react and reduces risk of stalemate (which is minimal here anyway). Since White only has a King, promoting any pawn should win easily.\n\nAnswer: P: c3-c2"}
