c2) attacks the king and forces it to move, possibly to f1 or d1, limiting its activity. Then we can follow up with king advance and pawn push. Alternatively, centralizing king to d4 or e4 helps control the board and support the knight.\n\nReason: Since we are up a knight and two pawns, simplification is fine but we should avoid stalemate traps. Moving the knight to c2 immediately pressures the white king and forces it into a more passive position (e.g., moving to f1 or d1). This gains tempo and allows our king to advance next move. Pawn moves are solid but less forcing; with such a material edge, active piece play is best. Among knight moves, N: e3-c2 is the most direct attack on the white king. King moves are safe but less aggressive. Therefore, N: e3-c2 is the best practical choice to increase pressure and create mating threats.\n\nAnswer: N: e3-c2'}
