hite king is at c1, moving the knight to a square that is not immediately capturable by the white king is wise.  \n\nLooking at the knight moves:  \n- **N: c2-a3** – Moves to a3, which is not adjacent to the white king (c1 to a3 is a knight move away, so safe from immediate capture). From a3, it can later move to b5 or c4, controlling squares and supporting pawn advance.  \n- **N: c2-b4** – Also safe, controls a6/c6 and can support pawn advance.  \n- **N: c2-d4** – Puts the knight closer to our king and central control, but white king could potentially threaten later.  \n\nHowever, our king is on e5 and can move to support the pawns as well. The most direct plan is to start pushing the a-pawns to queen. But moving the back pawn a7-a6 or a7-a5 is passive and doesn’t immediately create threats.  \n\nA good sequence: Move the knight to a safe square, then use king to support the a-pawn’s advance. The immediate move **N: c2-a3** is safe and also covers b5, which could be useful later. Alternatively, **K: e5-d6** centralizes the king toward the queenside to support pawn advance.  \n\nGiven we want to maximize winning chances, centralizing the king is often the most reliable in king+pawn endgames. **K: e5-d6** puts the king on d6, which:  \n- Protects the pawn on a4 indirectly (if white king approaches, black king can intercept).  \n- Prepares to support the a7 pawn’s advance to a5, a6, etc.  \n- The knight remains on c2 for now, guarding against white king coming to b3/d3.  \n\nThus, **K: e5-d6** seems the most solid and improves king activity for pawn promotion.\n\nAnswer: K: e5-d6"}
