ea, but less direct toward the pawns; keeps king near center.  \n3. **K: c5-c6** – King moves away from the action, not helpful.  \n4. **P: a5-a4** – Advances the lead pawn, but without king support it may become vulnerable later.  \n5. **P: a3-a2** – Advances the rear pawn to a2, but then it will be immediately attacked by White's king (Kb1-a2 or Kb1-a1) and likely lost unless our king can defend.\n\nReason: The key is to use the king to support the pawns. Moving the king to b4 is strong because:  \n- It places the king on the same file as the pawns (a-file) and one step closer to assist them.  \n- From b4, the king can go to a5 next move to push the a5 pawn, or to a3 to support the a3 pawn.  \n- It also cuts off the White king's direct path to the pawns (White king on b1 would have to go around via c2 or b2, giving us time).  \n- If we instead move a pawn immediately, we risk losing it without proper support. With only kings and pawns, the main plan is to create a passed pawn and escort it with the king. Here, the a-pawns are doubled, so we need to push the front one (a5) and possibly sacrifice the rear one (a3) as a decoy. Kc5-b4 starts that process effectively.\n\nThus, the best move is to centralize the king toward the pawns and the opponent's king.\n\nAnswer: K: c5-b4"}
