n's advance next turn. Best is probably to bring king ahead of pawn to escort it to promotion.  \n\nReason: The pawn is the only winning chance. We must promote it before White king catches it. The fastest promotion path is a3→a2→a1 (queen). But moving pawn immediately (a3-a2) leaves it on a2, and then Black king can move to b2 or a2 to defend. However, if king goes to a2, it blocks pawn's advance (pawn cannot move forward if king is in front). Better: King moves to b4 or a4 to cut off White king's route via b5/c5, while pawn stays on a3 ready to push next turn.  \n\nWhite king on d6: To reach a2 pawn after it moves to a2, White king needs at least 4 moves: d6→c5→b4→a3 (or d6→c5→b5→a4→a3, etc.). With Black king near, we can intercept.  \n\nBest candidate: K: b3-a4 — moves king closer to pawn’s promotion path and blocks White king’s approach via a5/b5. Then next turn push pawn to a2, then a1=Q with king defense.  \n\nOther moves like pawn move a3-a2 immediately also okay but then Black king might be less optimally placed for shielding. King to b4 is similar to a4 but a4 is more direct barrier.  \n\nAnswer: K: b3-a4"}
